        <h2>Food Safety Tips</h2>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Eat hot food:</strong> Wade just out of the fryer, kottu still steaming — hot street food is almost always safe.</li>
          <li><strong>Avoid cut fruit from carts:</strong> Freshly-cut fruit left out in the heat carries some risk. Whole fruit (thambili, bananas) is fine.</li>
          <li><strong>Drink bottled or filtered water:</strong> Don't drink tap water. Ice is generally made from filtered water in tourist areas but be cautious in local markets.</li>
          <li><strong>Right hand:</strong> If eating at a local table without cutlery, use your right hand. The left hand is considered unclean in South Asian culture.</li>
          <li><strong>Spice level:</strong> Sri Lankan street food is frequently hotter than restaurant food cooked for tourists. If your tongue suggests caution, it's probably right. The pol sambol especially.</li>
        </ul>

